  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nBehind the front, a surface anticyclone will expand into the Mid-\nMississippi River Valley Thursday and then arrive on Friday, \naccompanied by a seasonably cool and dry airmass (850-hPa \ntemperatures and PW around the 25th climatological percentile) \ninfiltrating the CWA. This airmass will translate to high \ntemperatures in the low to mid-80s F, low temperatures in the upper \n50s to mid-60s F, and lower humidity through Saturday morning. \nEfficient nighttime radiational cooling will also promote fog \ndevelopment each night/morning, predominantly in river valleys as a \ncombination of radiation and steam fog.\n\nThe surface anticyclone will quickly depart to the Great Lakes on \nSaturday as it follows the most vigorous subsidence leading an upper-\nlevel ridge amplifying and shifting into the Mid-Mississippi River \nValley. This process will facilitate southwesterly low-level \nflow/WAA with 850-hPa temperatures surging to around 21 to 23 C \n(above 95th climatological percentile) by Sunday, persisting into \nnext week. With full sunshine, these 850-hPa temperatures would \nlikely yield widespread high temperatures in the 90s F, including \nfavorable southwesterly flow and downslope warming off the Ozark \nPlateau potentially resulting in values even approaching 100 F in \ncentral MO and the St. Louis metro. Fortunately, dewpoints/humidity \nwill at least initially be lower than previous heatwaves we have \nseen so far this summer but probabilities of 100+ F heat index \nvalues do increase each day in response to a slow increase in \nhumidity/dewpoints. The details of the upper-level wave pattern next \nweek are still not uncertain but there has been an underlying trend \ntoward a stronger, more persistent ridge across the Mid-Mississippi \nRiver Valley. As such, confidence is increasing in a longer duration \nof heat through mid-next week. It is worth noting that there is a \nstill a preferred corridor for episodic showers and thunderstorms \naround the periphery of the ridge, but if this trend holds there is \nless concern for the CWA to be impacted by showers and thunderstorms \naside from far northern parts of northeastern MO and west-central IL \nthrough Wednesday.",
